<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gusinje</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gusinje is a small town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, close to the Albanian border in the Prokletije mountains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> awarded it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> to Montenegro in 1878, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> of the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> fought to keep them, and the great powers gave Montenegro the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a> instead in 1880. Montenegro gained Gusinje in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gusinje.jpg" alt="Gusinje" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Brož · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gusinje,_typicke_mestske_domy_z_pocatku_19._stol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gusinje lies at an elevation of 1,014 m in the Plav-Gusinje area of the upper Lim valley, in the Accursed Mountains. It stands on the Vermosh River, the first tributary of the Lim, which flows east towards Lake Plav. Zla Kolata, the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, rises about 10 km to the south in Prokletije National Park. The town is the seat of Gusinje Municipality, which covers 157 km2, much of it mountain land used in the past for herding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A medieval settlement stood here, and Gusinje was a caravan station on the route from Ragusa and Cattaro through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> to Peć in the 14th century. In 1485 Ottoman records list it as a village of 96 households in the imperial domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a>. The Venetian diplomat Mariano Bolizza reported that a fortress was finished late in 1612, built at the request of the Ottoman bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a> to check raids by the Kelmendi on the trade routes. Kelmendi and other highland groups later formed many of the town's neighbourhoods, and largely converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by the middle of the 18th century. In 1852 it had 1,500 households, 350 shops and five mosques.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> awarded Gusinje and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Albanians there protested and formed the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. Ali Pasha Shabanagaj, a landowner and commander from Gusinje, led its forces at the Battle of Novšiće, where they defeated a Montenegrin force under Marko Miljanov. More than 140 of about 300 League casualties came from Gusinje. The annexation was stopped and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a> was given to Montenegro as compensation.</p><p>Montenegrin forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> on 19 and 20 October 1912. The harsh military rule that followed caused, by one account in the sources, up to 1,800 killings and 12,000 forced conversions to Orthodoxy by March 1913. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> the town was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and its border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> was closed. In 1919 an Albanian revolt rose against inclusion in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and about 450 civilians were killed after it was put down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gusinje had 1,673 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and has been a municipality of its own again since 2014, after belonging to Plav Municipality from 1953. Most of its people are Muslim, speaking Albanian or a Slavic dialect that shows strong Albanian influence. The oldest surviving mosque is the Čekića džamija of 1687; the Vezir's mosque dates from 1765 and the New Mosque from 1899.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gusinje” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It fought Montenegro over Plav and <b>Gusinje</b> and Ulcinj.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-prizren">League of Prizren</a></li></ul></section>
